00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.519 align:start position:0%
critics and audiences agree that Aquaman  feels like a breath of fresh air after  drowning in DC's mercury our efforts  it's a light funny adventure film  anchored by two charming leads and  bullied by eye-popping visuals but a  vessel this large can only turn its  rudder so fast and it's bound to have a  few barnacles still clinging to its hull  prepare for spoilers and continue  full-speed ahead to the three best and  three worst things about Aquaman Batman  and Superman have some pretty iconic bad  guys always giving them grief that  contrasts Aquaman doesn't the movie  introduces perhaps the two most  significant villains from the pages of  Aquaman Comics Black Manta and ocean  master both of them are okay but the  story suffers slightly from having two  separate antagonists diluting the story  central conflict it's a problem all to  comment to superhero movies at least we  know we'll get more of Black Manta and  that big laser shooting helmet of his  and the inevitable sequel Jason Momoa  was a highlight of Justice League and  fans have been looking forward to more  of his likable dirtbag embodiment of  Arthur curry not only does the Aquaman  solo movie live up to its potential as a  Momoa vehicle it supports him with one  of the best casts yet assemble for the  DC EU it's an eclectic ensemble with  some surprising cameos but each gets a  moment to shine and unlike some of the  more convoluted comic movies in recent  memory everyone's clearly on the same  page about what kind of strange  adventure they're on Momoa continues his  shtick as the buff waterlogged Firefly  with a heart of gold but being the  center of attention allows him to add  new layers to the character he lets  Arthur self-doubt bubble to the surface  in his most vulnerable moments making  him endearing without sacrificing any of  his rowdy charm meanwhile Amber Heard  successfully leads alongside him as  meera with a perfect balance of fearsome  focus and wide-eyed wonder from her seen  wandering Sicily with Arthur to her  battle through the city Hurd makes mera  a true standout Justice League formally  introduced audiences to Aquaman but only  had time for a brief exploration of his  Atlantean origins mera  appeared in one sequence in which Arthur  attempted to protect a mother box from  Steppenwolf Arthur expressed bitterness  over being abandoned by his mother Queen  atlanna  and he'll turn neglect against the  people of Atlantis the Arthur we catch  up with an Aquaman is less angry at his  mother he's aware that she was forced to  return to the sea and learns that she  was sacrificed to a monster filled place  got the trench for the crime of having a  half-breed son filling him with grief  and guilt the movie more or less has to  undo the motivations established in  Justice League in order to get his  character arc moving in the right  direction there's also a change in how  Atlanteans communicate it seemed in  justice league that Arthur Amira could  only talk inside an air bubble but  Aquaman has its characters speak freely  even when submerged it makes sense that  a movie set mostly underwater would  choose not to complicate its characters  ability to talk to each other in this  way but it raises the question of why  Justice League introduced such a clunky  conceit in the first place  fans might be slightly annoyed at  Aquaman's contradictions of established  DC EU lore but this actually becomes one  of its strongest assets the movie is  completely unburdened by connections to  other movies either past or present  where Batman vs Superman dawn of Justice  nearly drowned in setups and  introductions designed to pay off in  future installments Aquaman floats free  unencumbered by the sort of franchise  minded filmmaking that has sunk other  comic-book movies  aside from briefly mentioning Arthur's  battle with Steppenwolf Aquaman contains  no reference to any other DC characters  not involved in its hero's journey no  secret Lex Corp files no foreshadowing  of cataclysmic events to come it's just  a straight ahead adventure story about  Aquaman and his friends and enemies it  is in fact a perfect jumping on point  for newcomers to the DC Universe  something Warner Brothers sorely needs  after the convoluted webs of its  predecessors proved off-putting for  audiences and critics regardless of how  well Aquaman plays to its strengths it's  still going to be the story of a  would-be king who talks to fish and does  battle with merpeople it will probably  never reach the universal acclaim of  something like The Dark Knight a movie  based on comic books and treated as a  hard-hitting police drama  Arthur Curry's adventures will simply  never have the same potential for  crossover appeal but here's the thing  about a movie that leans all the way  into its own comic book silliness it's  exactly what DC films needs right now  while some discerning theater goers  might be turned off by such a goofy  outing as Aquaman it serves as the  perfect antidote to the grim self  seriousness of movies like Batman vs  Superman tell me  we've watched Superman die and we've  seen Batman live long enough to become  the villain even Wonder Woman solo story  didn't have much time for levity once  Diana arrived in the trenches of World  War one  Justice League had a jarring tonal  dissonance trapped somewhere between  brooding and romping pitchfork  but Aquaman is bright wacky and seems  intent on doing nothing beyond shooting  fun straight into viewers eyeballs  Arthur's tale is a feel-good story  concerned not with societal ethics or  the meaning of existence but with his  own journey to find his own place and  commit to what he cares about it will  never make a short list of important  films or warrant major oscar  consideration but it isn't trying to  it's just fun and in a year when Marvel  slaughtered half of all life in the  universe that makes it a welcome dash of  lightness and joy sure I just peed on it  [Music]
 

